ect to the whole area of a “peer review”.  It is supposed to be anonymous but often is not.  It may seem opaque to outsiders, but insiders often know who is who, and who reviews for what publication because people put it on their resumes.  Further, anyone who reads any academic publication with any regularity quickly becomes aware of editorial prejudices and commonalities for a given publication, and therefore knows exactly which publication to which to submit a particular kind of academic paper in hopes of being published.\n\nAnother way of saying this is simpler and blunter.\n\nAbout half of academia is fraud or fraudulent in someway.  \n\nIt is worse outside of STEM, of course, but anything that involves human studies and control groups has to be replicated by a completely disinterested set of researchers with no connection to the originators of the research in order to be remotely trustworthy.\n\nThere are just too many examples of one shot fraud, or overdrawing conclusions from ambiguous data as if they are now certainties.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 30 18:59:28 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1884679638926110763', 'display_text_range': [14, 284], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 3, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd This sort of thing infects every level of academia that is publish or perish driven.  There is definitely a “you scratch my back and I’ll scratch yours” aspect to the whole area of a “peer review”.  It is supposed to be anonymous but often is not.  It may seem opaque to', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1884679638926110763', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': Fals